{
  "dom_id": "step-e13256",
  "name": "Example: Finding a Ratio",
  "problem": "What ratio describes the number of pentagons to the total number of shapes?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14133305/assets/0fff5dd90ce40b76128001c25b41cf8f34a2b4f63c44717823ba0bdcc98ef224.png)",
  "worked_solution": "We can express the ratio of the number of pentagons to the total number of shapes as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{number of pentagons} : \\textrm{total number of shapes}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of pentagons. There are $3$ pentagons.\n\n- Next, we count the total number of shapes. There are $6$ shapes.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of pentagons to total shapes is $3:6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
